[2026-01-30T00:15:22.692Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgCxYHB9Vu1vzV5W6MHcTn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# 🔍 Claude Code Idea Generation: Feature Scout\n     2→\n     3→## Mission\n     4→You are tasked with generating high-quality backlog ideas for the \"res\" project.\n     5→Your role is: **Feature Scout**\n     6→\n     7→\n     8→## Target Context\n     9→- Context ID: ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\n    10→- Context Name: Report Theming & Cards\n    11→\n    12→\n    13→---\n    14→\n    15→## CRITICAL: Understanding Your Task\n    16→\n    17→**IMPORTANT DISTINCTION - READ CAREFULLY:**\n    18→\n    19→1. **ANALYZE**: The \"res\" project (the codebase you're exploring)\n    20→2. **SAVE TO**: Vibeman's idea management database (a SEPARATE system at http://localhost:3000)\n    21→\n    22→You are NOT creating API endpoints. You are NOT modifying the target project's code.\n    23→You are ANALYZING the target project and SAVING your findings to Vibeman's external API.\n    24→\n    25→The /api/scans and /api/ideas endpoints below are **Vibeman's management APIs** - they already exist.\n    26→Do NOT attempt to create these endpoints in the \"res\" project.\n    27→\n    28→Your job is:\n    29→- READ and ANALYZE the \"res\" codebase\n    30→- GENERATE ideas based on your analysis\n    31→- SAVE those ideas by calling Vibeman's existing APIs via curl\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→## Analysis Prompt\n    36→\n    37→Below is the specialized analysis prompt for this scan type. Use this to guide your analysis:\n    38→\n    39→---\n    40→\n    41→You are the **Feature Scout** — an expert analyst of successful software products who identifies proven features missing from a specific context within the \"res\" project.\n    42→\n    43→## Your Expertise\n    44→\n    45→You have deep knowledge of **established, successful products** across every category — project management tools (Notion, Linear, Jira), productivity apps (Todoist, Things, Obsidian), developer tools (GitHub, VS Code, Figma), and countless others. You know which features made these products beloved and why.\n    46→\n    47→Your superpower is **pattern recognition across products**. When you see an app, you immediately recognize: \"This is similar to X, but it's missing features Y and Z that made X great.\" You don't just copy — you identify what's proven and then push it one step further.\n    48→\n    49→## Your Mission\n    50→\n    51→**Bridge the feature gap.** You're here to:\n    52→\n    53→- Identify what category of established products this app competes with\n    54→- Recognize which proven, expected features are conspicuously absent\n    55→- Propose those features with adaptations specific to THIS app's context\n    56→- Push each borrowed idea one step further — make it even better than the original\n    57→\n    58→You draw from your training on thousands of successful products. You know what users expect because you know what they've experienced elsewhere.\n    59→\n    60→## Feature Discovery Dimensions\n    61→\n    62→### 🏆 Industry Standards\n    63→- **Table Stakes Features**: What do ALL successful products in this category have?\n    64→- **User Expectations**: What will users look for and be disappointed not to find?\n    65→- **Competitive Parity**: What features make competitors successful?\n    66→- **Platform Conventions**: What do users expect from apps on this platform?\n    67→\n    68→### 🚀 Best-in-Class Inspiration\n    69→Draw from your deep knowledge of successful software products. You've been trained on documentation, reviews, and discussions of thousands of applications across every domain. Use this knowledge to:\n    70→\n    71→- **Identify Analogous Products**: What successful products solve similar problems? What made them beloved?\n    72→- **Extract Winning Patterns**: Which features consistently delight users across different products in this category?\n    73→- **Recognize Feature Evolution**: How have these patterns evolved? What's the state of the art?\n    74→- **Cross-Pollinate**: What features from adjacent categories could translate brilliantly here?\n    75→\n    76→Your training contains the collective wisdom of software design. Surface the patterns that work.\n    77→\n    78→### 🔮 One Step Further\n    79→- **Enhancement**: How could this feature be BETTER than the original?\n    80→- **Integration**: How does it combine uniquely with THIS app's capabilities?\n    81→- **Simplification**: Can the proven concept be made more elegant here?\n    82→- **Innovation**: What's the next evolution of this feature category?\n    83→\n    84→### 🎯 Fit Assessment\n    85→- **Relevance**: Does this feature serve THIS app's core purpose?\n    86→- **Feasibility**: Can it be built with the existing architecture?\n    87→- **Value Density**: High impact relative to implementation effort?\n    88→- **User Need**: Would users of THIS app actually want this?\n    89→\n    90→## CRITICAL: JSON Output Format\n    91→\n    92→**You MUST respond with ONLY a valid JSON array. Follow these rules EXACTLY:**\n    93→\n    94→1. ❌ NO markdown code blocks (no ```json or ```)\n    95→2. ❌ NO explanatory text before or after the JSON\n    96→3. ❌ NO comments in the JSON\n    97→4. ✅ ONLY pure JSON array starting with [ and ending with ]\n    98→\n    99→**Expected JSON structure (copy this structure exactly):**\n   100→\n   101→[\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n   104→    \"title\": \"Short, descriptive title (max 60 characters)\",\n   105→    \"description\": \"Detailed explanation of the idea, what it solves, and how it helps (2-4 sentences). Be specific about implementation approach.\",\n   106→    \"reasoning\": \"Why this idea is valuable. What problem does it solve? What's the impact? (2-3 sentences).\",\n   107→    \"effort\": 2,\n   108→    \"impact\": 3\n   109→  }\n   110→]\n   111→\n   112→### Field Requirements:\n   113→\n   114→**REQUIRED FIELDS** (must be present in every idea):\n   115→- `title`: string (max 60 chars, clear and specific)\n   116→- `category`: string (one of the valid categories for your scan type)\n   117→- `description`: string (2-4 sentences, implementation-focused)\n   118→- `reasoning`: string (2-3 sentences, value-focused)\n   119→\n   120→**STRONGLY RECOMMENDED FIELDS** (should always be included):\n   121→- `effort`: number (1, 2, or 3 - implementation difficulty)\n   122→- `impact`: number (1, 2, or 3 - value to project)\n   123→\n   124→### Effort and Impact Ratings:\n   125→\n   126→**Effort** (Implementation difficulty):\n   127→- **1** = Low effort (Quick fix, minor change, 1-2 hours)\n   128→- **2** = Medium effort (Moderate change, requires planning, 1-2 days)\n   129→- **3** = High effort (Major change, significant refactoring, 1+ weeks)\n   130→\n   131→**Impact** (Value to project):\n   132→- **1** = Low impact (Nice to have, minor improvement)\n   133→- **2** = Medium impact (Noticeable improvement, good value)\n   134→- **3** = High impact (Game changer, major value, critical improvement)\n   135→\n   136→### Valid Categories:\n   137→- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n   138→- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n   139→- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n   140→- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, accessibility, responsiveness\n   141→- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n   142→- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n   143→\n   144→---\n   145→\n   146→### Valid Categories for This Scan:\n   147→- **functionality**: New features, capabilities, extensions, integrations\n   148→- **user_benefit**: User value, business impact, workflow improvements\n   149→\n   150→### Your Standards:\n   151→1.  **Proven Success**: Reference the pattern or category of products where this feature thrives\n   152→2.  **Adaptation**: Explain how it fits THIS app specifically\n   153→3.  **Enhancement**: Describe how to push it one step further\n   154→4.  **Implementation Path**: How to build this using existing architecture\n   155→\n   156→---\n   157→\n   158→\n   159→\n   160→## Context Information\n   161→\n   162→**Context Name**: Report Theming & Cards\n   163→\n   164→**Context Description**:\n   165→Themed UI components for reports: stat cards, sections, badges, and core elements. Includes animated numbers and theme context for consistent styling.\n   166→\n   167→**Files in this Context** (9 files):\n   168→- src/components/report/ThemedCards.tsx\n   169→- src/components/report/core/ThemeContext.tsx\n   170→- src/components/report/core/AnimatedNumber.tsx\n   171→- src/components/report/core/index.tsx\n   172→- src/components/report/shared/Cards.tsx\n   173→- src/components/report/shared/Badges.tsx\n   174→- src/components/report/shared/ProgressBar.tsx\n   175→- src/components/report/shared/Icons.tsx\n   176→- src/components/report/shared/index.tsx\n   177→\n   178→\n   179→\n   180→## Existing Ideas\n   181→\n   182→No pending or accepted ideas found. This is a fresh analysis.\n   183→\n   184→\n   185→\n   186→\n   187→\n   188→---\n   189→\n   190→## Your Process\n   191→\n   192→1.  **Categorize**: What type of product is this? What category does it compete in?\n   193→2.  **Benchmark**: What are the best products in this category? What makes them great?\n   194→3.  **Gap Analysis**: What proven features are missing that users will expect?\n   195→4.  **Enhance**: How can each borrowed feature be made even better here?\n   196→\n   197→### Champion:\n   198→- Features proven successful in similar products, adapted to this context\n   199→- \"Why don't we have this?\" features users expect from the category\n   200→- Enhancements that make the borrowed idea even better than the original\n   201→- Features that combine proven patterns with this app's unique capabilities\n   202→\n   203→### Reject:\n   204→- Features that don't fit this app's purpose or user base\n   205→- Direct copies without adaptation or enhancement\n   206→- Features from unrelated product categories\n   207→- Trends without proven user value\n   208→\n   209→### Expected Output:\n   210→Generate 3-5 **STRATEGIC** feature ideas. Each should be inspired by proven patterns from successful products in relevant categories, adapted to this app's context, and pushed one step further. Describe the pattern's origins and why it succeeds.\n   211→\n   212→\n   213→**Feature Gap Analysis**:\n   214→The context described above is your analysis target.\n   215→- What established products have similar functionality?\n   216→- What features do THOSE products have that's missing here?\n   217→- How would those features be enhanced for THIS app?\n   218→- What would make users say \"finally, they added X\"?\n   219→\n   220→\n   221→\n   222→---\n   223→\n   224→## ⚠️ FINAL REMINDER: OUTPUT FORMAT\n   225→\n   226→Your response must be ONLY a JSON array. Here's what your response should look like:\n   227→\n   228→[{\"category\":\"functionality\",\"title\":\"Add user profile caching\",\"description\":\"Implement Redis caching for user profile data to reduce database queries. Cache should invalidate on profile updates and have a 5-minute TTL. This will significantly reduce load on the users table.\",\"reasoning\":\"User profiles are accessed on every page load but rarely change. Caching reduces DB load by ~80% and improves page load times. High impact for minimal implementation effort.\",\"effort\":1,\"impact\":3}]\n   229→\n   230→❌ DO NOT wrap in markdown:\n   231→```json\n   232→[...]\n   233→```\n   234→\n   235→❌ DO NOT add explanations:\n   236→Here are the ideas:\n   237→[...]\n   238→\n   239→✅ ONLY output the JSON array, nothing else. Generate as many high-quality ideas as you believe would genuinely push this project to the next level - focus on quality and actionability over quantity.\n   240→\n   241→\n   242→## Project Goals\n   243→\n   244→No open goals found for this project.\n   245→\n   246→\n   247→\n   248→---\n   249→\n   250→## Saving Ideas to Vibeman's Database\n   251→\n   252→**Note:** These API calls go to Vibeman's idea management system (http://localhost:3000), NOT the project you're analyzing.\n   253→\n   254→You need to perform TWO steps to save ideas:\n   255→\n   256→### Step 1: Create a Scan Record\n   257→First, create a scan record to track this idea generation session.\n   258→\n   259→```bash\n   260→curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n   261→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   262→  -d '{\n   263→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   264→    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_feature_scout\",\n   265→    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Feature Scout\"\n   266→  }'\n   267→```\n   268→\n   269→The response will include a `scan.id` - save this for the next step.\n   270→\n   271→### Step 2: Create Ideas\n   272→For each idea, make a POST request with this JSON body:\n   273→\n   274→```\n   275→POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas\n   276→Content-Type: application/json\n   277→\n   278→{\n   279→  \"scan_id\": \"<scan_id_from_step_1>\",\n   280→  \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   281→  \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n   282→  \"scan_type\": \"feature_scout\",\n   283→  \"category\": \"<category>\",\n   284→  \"title\": \"<title>\",\n   285→  \"description\": \"<description>\",\n   286→  \"reasoning\": \"<reasoning>\",\n   287→  \"effort\": <1-10>,\n   288→  \"impact\": <1-10>,\n   289→  \"risk\": <1-10>,\n   290→  \"goal_id\": \"<goal_id_if_matched>\"\n   291→}\n   292→```\n   293→\n   294→**IMPORTANT:** Always include effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10) for every idea. Do NOT leave these fields empty or null.\n   295→\n   296→### Field Requirements\n   297→\n   298→**category** (string): One of:\n   299→- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n   300→- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n   301→- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n   302→- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, responsiveness\n   303→- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n   304→- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n   305→\n   306→**title** (string, max 60 chars): Clear, specific, action-oriented title\n   307→\n   308→**description** (string): 2-4 sentences explaining:\n   309→- What the idea is\n   310→- How it would be implemented\n   311→- What problem it solves\n   312→\n   313→**reasoning** (string): 2-3 sentences explaining:\n   314→- Why this idea is valuable\n   315→- What impact it will have\n   316→- Why now is a good time to implement it\n   317→\n   318→**effort** (number 1-10) - Total cost to deliver: time, complexity, people, and coordination overhead:\n   319→- 1-2 = Trivial (few hours to a day, single file/config change, no coordination)\n   320→- 3-4 = Small (few days, localized to one module, minimal testing)\n   321→- 5-6 = Medium (1-2 weeks, multiple components, requires thoughtful testing)\n   322→- 7-8 = Large (several weeks to a month, spans multiple services, requires coordination)\n   323→- 9-10 = Massive (multi-month initiative, dedicated team, new architecture)\n   324→\n   325→**impact** (number 1-10) - Business value, user satisfaction, and strategic alignment:\n   326→- 1-2 = Negligible (nice-to-have, no measurable user/business outcome)\n   327→- 3-4 = Minor (quality-of-life for small user subset, weak strategy alignment)\n   328→- 5-6 = Moderate (clear benefit to meaningful segment OR solid OKR alignment)\n   329→- 7-8 = High (strong user impact across significant portion of base, clear competitive/revenue implication)\n   330→- 9-10 = Critical (existential for product success, major revenue driver, transformational work)\n   331→\n   332→**risk** (number 1-10) - Probability and severity of things going wrong:\n   333→- 1-2 = Very safe (well-understood change, easily reversible, no security/data/compliance surface)\n   334→- 3-4 = Low risk (minor uncertainty, limited blast radius, standard rollback possible)\n   335→- 5-6 = Moderate (some technical unknowns OR touches sensitive area like payments/auth/PII)\n   336→- 7-8 = High (significant uncertainty, depends on external systems, potential user-facing regression)\n   337→- 9-10 = Critical (novel/unproven approach, hard to reverse, major outage/data loss potential)\n   338→\n   339→**goal_id** (optional string): If the idea relates to one of the project goals listed above, include the goal ID\n   340→\n   341→## Example Workflow\n   342→\n   343→```bash\n   344→# Step 1: Create scan record\n   345→SCAN_RESPONSE=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n   346→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   347→  -d '{\n   348→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   349→    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_feature_scout\",\n   350→    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Feature Scout\"\n   351→  }')\n   352→\n   353→# Extract scan_id from response\n   354→SCAN_ID=$(echo $SCAN_RESPONSE | jq -r '.scan.id')\n   355→\n   356→# Step 2: Create ideas using the scan_id\n   357→curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas \\\n   358→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   359→  -d '{\n   360→    \"scan_id\": \"'$SCAN_ID'\",\n   361→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   362→    \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n   363→    \"scan_type\": \"feature_scout\",\n   364→    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n   365→    \"title\": \"Example: Add user session caching layer\",\n   366→    \"description\": \"Implement Redis caching for user session data to reduce database queries. This would cache session info for 5 minutes with automatic invalidation on updates.\",\n   367→    \"reasoning\": \"Currently every page load queries the session table. This adds latency and database load. Caching would reduce DB calls by ~70%.\",\n   368→    \"effort\": 5,\n   369→    \"impact\": 7,\n   370→    \"risk\": 4\n   371→  }'\n   372→```\n   373→\n   374→## Execution Steps\n   375→\n   376→**Phase 1: Analyze the \"res\" project**\n   377→1. Read the project's CLAUDE.md or AI.md documentation if available\n   378→2. Explore the codebase structure, focusing on the context files\n   379→3. Analyze code with the perspective described in the analysis prompt above\n   380→4. Generate high-quality ideas that would genuinely push this project forward\n   381→\n   382→**Phase 2: Save ideas to Vibeman (external system at http://localhost:3000)**\n   383→5. Create a scan record via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/scans\n   384→6. Save each idea via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/ideas using the scan_id\n   385→7. Report what ideas were created\n   386→\n   387→**REMINDER:** Do NOT create any files or endpoints in \"res\". Only READ/ANALYZE it.\n   388→\n   389→## Quality Standards\n   390→\n   391→- **Be Specific**: Reference actual files, components, or patterns you observed\n   392→- **Be Actionable**: Ideas should be clear enough to implement without further clarification\n   393→- **Be Valuable**: Focus on ideas that bring real improvement, not busywork\n   394→- **Match Goals**: If an idea aligns with a project goal, include the goal_id\n   395→- **Avoid Duplicates**: Check the existing ideas section and don't suggest similar items\n   396→\n   397→## Output\n   398→\n   399→After completing the task, summarize:\n   400→- How many ideas were created (saved to Vibeman at http://localhost:3000)\n   401→- Brief list of idea titles\n   402→- Any observations about the \"res\" codebase\n   403→\n   404→**Final Checklist:**\n   405→- [ ] I analyzed the \"res\" codebase (READ ONLY)\n   406→- [ ] I did NOT create any new files in \"res\"\n   407→- [ ] I saved ideas via curl to Vibeman's API at http://localhost:3000\n   408→- [ ] Each idea has effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10)\n   409→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05d3e4f1-2259-4f3e-a055-8040a34c8b02","uuid":"ee4e8ce2-83b7-40ca-a0b0-2547ee172791","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\mkdol\\dolla\\res\\.claude\\commands\\idea-gen-1769729285632-ctx_1769-fs.md","content":"# 🔍 Claude Code Idea Generation: Feature Scout\n\n## Mission\nYou are tasked with generating high-quality backlog ideas for the \"res\" project.\nYour role is: **Feature Scout**\n\n\n## Target Context\n- Context ID: ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\n- Context Name: Report Theming & Cards\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Understanding Your Task\n\n**IMPORTANT DISTINCTION - READ CAREFULLY:**\n\n1. **ANALYZE**: The \"res\" project (the codebase you're exploring)\n2. **SAVE TO**: Vibeman's idea management database (a SEPARATE system at http://localhost:3000)\n\nYou are NOT creating API endpoints. You are NOT modifying the target project's code.\nYou are ANALYZING the target project and SAVING your findings to Vibeman's external API.\n\nThe /api/scans and /api/ideas endpoints below are **Vibeman's management APIs** - they already exist.\nDo NOT attempt to create these endpoints in the \"res\" project.\n\nYour job is:\n- READ and ANALYZE the \"res\" codebase\n- GENERATE ideas based on your analysis\n- SAVE those ideas by calling Vibeman's existing APIs via curl\n\n---\n\n## Analysis Prompt\n\nBelow is the specialized analysis prompt for this scan type. Use this to guide your analysis:\n\n---\n\nYou are the **Feature Scout** — an expert analyst of successful software products who identifies proven features missing from a specific context within the \"res\" project.\n\n## Your Expertise\n\nYou have deep knowledge of **established, successful products** across every category — project management tools (Notion, Linear, Jira), productivity apps (Todoist, Things, Obsidian), developer tools (GitHub, VS Code, Figma), and countless others. You know which features made these products beloved and why.\n\nYour superpower is **pattern recognition across products**. When you see an app, you immediately recognize: \"This is similar to X, but it's missing features Y and Z that made X great.\" You don't just copy — you identify what's proven and then push it one step further.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n**Bridge the feature gap.** You're here to:\n\n- Identify what category of established products this app competes with\n- Recognize which proven, expected features are conspicuously absent\n- Propose those features with adaptations specific to THIS app's context\n- Push each borrowed idea one step further — make it even better than the original\n\nYou draw from your training on thousands of successful products. You know what users expect because you know what they've experienced elsewhere.\n\n## Feature Discovery Dimensions\n\n### 🏆 Industry Standards\n- **Table Stakes Features**: What do ALL successful products in this category have?\n- **User Expectations**: What will users look for and be disappointed not to find?\n- **Competitive Parity**: What features make competitors successful?\n- **Platform Conventions**: What do users expect from apps on this platform?\n\n### 🚀 Best-in-Class Inspiration\nDraw from your deep knowledge of successful software products. You've been trained on documentation, reviews, and discussions of thousands of applications across every domain. Use this knowledge to:\n\n- **Identify Analogous Products**: What successful products solve similar problems? What made them beloved?\n- **Extract Winning Patterns**: Which features consistently delight users across different products in this category?\n- **Recognize Feature Evolution**: How have these patterns evolved? What's the state of the art?\n- **Cross-Pollinate**: What features from adjacent categories could translate brilliantly here?\n\nYour training contains the collective wisdom of software design. Surface the patterns that work.\n\n### 🔮 One Step Further\n- **Enhancement**: How could this feature be BETTER than the original?\n- **Integration**: How does it combine uniquely with THIS app's capabilities?\n- **Simplification**: Can the proven concept be made more elegant here?\n- **Innovation**: What's the next evolution of this feature category?\n\n### 🎯 Fit Assessment\n- **Relevance**: Does this feature serve THIS app's core purpose?\n- **Feasibility**: Can it be built with the existing architecture?\n- **Value Density**: High impact relative to implementation effort?\n- **User Need**: Would users of THIS app actually want this?\n\n## CRITICAL: JSON Output Format\n\n**You MUST respond with ONLY a valid JSON array. Follow these rules EXACTLY:**\n\n1. ❌ NO markdown code blocks (no ```json or ```)\n2. ❌ NO explanatory text before or after the JSON\n3. ❌ NO comments in the JSON\n4. ✅ ONLY pure JSON array starting with [ and ending with ]\n\n**Expected JSON structure (copy this structure exactly):**\n\n[\n  {\n    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n    \"title\": \"Short, descriptive title (max 60 characters)\",\n    \"description\": \"Detailed explanation of the idea, what it solves, and how it helps (2-4 sentences). Be specific about implementation approach.\",\n    \"reasoning\": \"Why this idea is valuable. What problem does it solve? What's the impact? (2-3 sentences).\",\n    \"effort\": 2,\n    \"impact\": 3\n  }\n]\n\n### Field Requirements:\n\n**REQUIRED FIELDS** (must be present in every idea):\n- `title`: string (max 60 chars, clear and specific)\n- `category`: string (one of the valid categories for your scan type)\n- `description`: string (2-4 sentences, implementation-focused)\n- `reasoning`: string (2-3 sentences, value-focused)\n\n**STRONGLY RECOMMENDED FIELDS** (should always be included):\n- `effort`: number (1, 2, or 3 - implementation difficulty)\n- `impact`: number (1, 2, or 3 - value to project)\n\n### Effort and Impact Ratings:\n\n**Effort** (Implementation difficulty):\n- **1** = Low effort (Quick fix, minor change, 1-2 hours)\n- **2** = Medium effort (Moderate change, requires planning, 1-2 days)\n- **3** = High effort (Major change, significant refactoring, 1+ weeks)\n\n**Impact** (Value to project):\n- **1** = Low impact (Nice to have, minor improvement)\n- **2** = Medium impact (Noticeable improvement, good value)\n- **3** = High impact (Game changer, major value, critical improvement)\n\n### Valid Categories:\n- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, accessibility, responsiveness\n- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n\n---\n\n### Valid Categories for This Scan:\n- **functionality**: New features, capabilities, extensions, integrations\n- **user_benefit**: User value, business impact, workflow improvements\n\n### Your Standards:\n1.  **Proven Success**: Reference the pattern or category of products where this feature thrives\n2.  **Adaptation**: Explain how it fits THIS app specifically\n3.  **Enhancement**: Describe how to push it one step further\n4.  **Implementation Path**: How to build this using existing architecture\n\n---\n\n\n\n## Context Information\n\n**Context Name**: Report Theming & Cards\n\n**Context Description**:\nThemed UI components for reports: stat cards, sections, badges, and core elements. Includes animated numbers and theme context for consistent styling.\n\n**Files in this Context** (9 files):\n- src/components/report/ThemedCards.tsx\n- src/components/report/core/ThemeContext.tsx\n- src/components/report/core/AnimatedNumber.tsx\n- src/components/report/core/index.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/Cards.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/Badges.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/ProgressBar.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/Icons.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/index.tsx\n\n\n\n## Existing Ideas\n\nNo pending or accepted ideas found. This is a fresh analysis.\n\n\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Your Process\n\n1.  **Categorize**: What type of product is this? What category does it compete in?\n2.  **Benchmark**: What are the best products in this category? What makes them great?\n3.  **Gap Analysis**: What proven features are missing that users will expect?\n4.  **Enhance**: How can each borrowed feature be made even better here?\n\n### Champion:\n- Features proven successful in similar products, adapted to this context\n- \"Why don't we have this?\" features users expect from the category\n- Enhancements that make the borrowed idea even better than the original\n- Features that combine proven patterns with this app's unique capabilities\n\n### Reject:\n- Features that don't fit this app's purpose or user base\n- Direct copies without adaptation or enhancement\n- Features from unrelated product categories\n- Trends without proven user value\n\n### Expected Output:\nGenerate 3-5 **STRATEGIC** feature ideas. Each should be inspired by proven patterns from successful products in relevant categories, adapted to this app's context, and pushed one step further. Describe the pattern's origins and why it succeeds.\n\n\n**Feature Gap Analysis**:\nThe context described above is your analysis target.\n- What established products have similar functionality?\n- What features do THOSE products have that's missing here?\n- How would those features be enhanced for THIS app?\n- What would make users say \"finally, they added X\"?\n\n\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ FINAL REMINDER: OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYour response must be ONLY a JSON array. Here's what your response should look like:\n\n[{\"category\":\"functionality\",\"title\":\"Add user profile caching\",\"description\":\"Implement Redis caching for user profile data to reduce database queries. Cache should invalidate on profile updates and have a 5-minute TTL. This will significantly reduce load on the users table.\",\"reasoning\":\"User profiles are accessed on every page load but rarely change. Caching reduces DB load by ~80% and improves page load times. High impact for minimal implementation effort.\",\"effort\":1,\"impact\":3}]\n\n❌ DO NOT wrap in markdown:\n```json\n[...]\n```\n\n❌ DO NOT add explanations:\nHere are the ideas:\n[...]\n\n✅ ONLY output the JSON array, nothing else. Generate as many high-quality ideas as you believe would genuinely push this project to the next level - focus on quality and actionability over quantity.\n\n\n## Project Goals\n\nNo open goals found for this project.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Saving Ideas to Vibeman's Database\n\n**Note:** These API calls go to Vibeman's idea management system (http://localhost:3000), NOT the project you're analyzing.\n\nYou need to perform TWO steps to save ideas:\n\n### Step 1: Create a Scan Record\nFirst, create a scan record to track this idea generation session.\n\n```bash\ncurl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_feature_scout\",\n    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Feature Scout\"\n  }'\n```\n\nThe response will include a `scan.id` - save this for the next step.\n\n### Step 2: Create Ideas\nFor each idea, make a POST request with this JSON body:\n\n```\nPOST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"scan_id\": \"<scan_id_from_step_1>\",\n  \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n  \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n  \"scan_type\": \"feature_scout\",\n  \"category\": \"<category>\",\n  \"title\": \"<title>\",\n  \"description\": \"<description>\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"<reasoning>\",\n  \"effort\": <1-10>,\n  \"impact\": <1-10>,\n  \"risk\": <1-10>,\n  \"goal_id\": \"<goal_id_if_matched>\"\n}\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT:** Always include effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10) for every idea. Do NOT leave these fields empty or null.\n\n### Field Requirements\n\n**category** (string): One of:\n- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, responsiveness\n- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n\n**title** (string, max 60 chars): Clear, specific, action-oriented title\n\n**description** (string): 2-4 sentences explaining:\n- What the idea is\n- How it would be implemented\n- What problem it solves\n\n**reasoning** (string): 2-3 sentences explaining:\n- Why this idea is valuable\n- What impact it will have\n- Why now is a good time to implement it\n\n**effort** (number 1-10) - Total cost to deliver: time, complexity, people, and coordination overhead:\n- 1-2 = Trivial (few hours to a day, single file/config change, no coordination)\n- 3-4 = Small (few days, localized to one module, minimal testing)\n- 5-6 = Medium (1-2 weeks, multiple components, requires thoughtful testing)\n- 7-8 = Large (several weeks to a month, spans multiple services, requires coordination)\n- 9-10 = Massive (multi-month initiative, dedicated team, new architecture)\n\n**impact** (number 1-10) - Business value, user satisfaction, and strategic alignment:\n- 1-2 = Negligible (nice-to-have, no measurable user/business outcome)\n- 3-4 = Minor (quality-of-life for small user subset, weak strategy alignment)\n- 5-6 = Moderate (clear benefit to meaningful segment OR solid OKR alignment)\n- 7-8 = High (strong user impact across significant portion of base, clear competitive/revenue implication)\n- 9-10 = Critical (existential for product success, major revenue driver, transformational work)\n\n**risk** (number 1-10) - Probability and severity of things going wrong:\n- 1-2 = Very safe (well-understood change, easily reversible, no security/data/compliance surface)\n- 3-4 = Low risk (minor uncertainty, limited blast radius, standard rollback possible)\n- 5-6 = Moderate (some technical unknowns OR touches sensitive area like payments/auth/PII)\n- 7-8 = High (significant uncertainty, depends on external systems, potential user-facing regression)\n- 9-10 = Critical (novel/unproven approach, hard to reverse, major outage/data loss potential)\n\n**goal_id** (optional string): If the idea relates to one of the project goals listed above, include the goal ID\n\n## Example Workflow\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Create scan record\nSCAN_RESPONSE=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_feature_scout\",\n    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Feature Scout\"\n  }')\n\n# Extract scan_id from response\nSCAN_ID=$(echo $SCAN_RESPONSE | jq -r '.scan.id')\n\n# Step 2: Create ideas using the scan_id\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"scan_id\": \"'$SCAN_ID'\",\n    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n    \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n    \"scan_type\": \"feature_scout\",\n    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n    \"title\": \"Example: Add user session caching layer\",\n    \"description\": \"Implement Redis caching for user session data to reduce database queries. This would cache session info for 5 minutes with automatic invalidation on updates.\",\n    \"reasoning\": \"Currently every page load queries the session table. This adds latency and database load. Caching would reduce DB calls by ~70%.\",\n    \"effort\": 5,\n    \"impact\": 7,\n    \"risk\": 4\n  }'\n```\n\n## Execution Steps\n\n**Phase 1: Analyze the \"res\" project**\n1. Read the project's CLAUDE.md or AI.md documentation if available\n2. Explore the codebase structure, focusing on the context files\n3. Analyze code with the perspective described in the analysis prompt above\n4. Generate high-quality ideas that would genuinely push this project forward\n\n**Phase 2: Save ideas to Vibeman (external system at http://localhost:3000)**\n5. Create a scan record via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/scans\n6. Save each idea via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/ideas using the scan_id\n7. Report what ideas were created\n\n**REMINDER:** Do NOT create any files or endpoints in \"res\". Only READ/ANALYZE it.\n\n## Quality Standards\n\n- **Be Specific**: Reference actual files, components, or patterns you observed\n- **Be Actionable**: Ideas should be clear enough to implement without further clarification\n- **Be Valuable**: Focus on ideas that bring real improvement, not busywork\n- **Match Goals**: If an idea aligns with a project goal, include the goal_id\n- **Avoid Duplicates**: Check the existing ideas section and don't suggest similar items\n\n## Output\n\nAfter completing the task, summarize:\n- How many ideas were created (saved to Vibeman at http://localhost:3000)\n- Brief list of idea titles\n- Any observations about the \"res\" codebase\n\n**Final Checklist:**\n- [ ] I analyzed the \"res\" codebase (READ ONLY)\n- [ ] I did NOT create any new files in \"res\"\n- [ ] I saved ideas via curl to Vibeman's API at http://localhost:3000\n- [ ] Each idea has effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10)\n","numLines":409,"startLine":1,"totalLines":409}}}
